I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the reusable refinement utilities first. Then I’ll screen candidate phases, audit structural models, and refine competing complete-pattern models with stable checkpoints before exporting the required files.

The metadata points to a Zn–W–O reaction measured with Cu Kα1/Kα2 from 9.9–100.1° 2θ. I’m now extracting peak positions and querying the masked structure library for source-supported Zn/W/O candidates (including possible unreacted oxides), rather than assuming the nominal reaction product.

The audited ZnWO₄ wolframite model is chemically sound and its calculated reflections match the observed peak family across the full range, including the distinctive 18.98°, 23.87/24.62°, 30.69°, 41.39°, 51.86°, 61.90°, and 64.93° features. ZnO and WO₃ remain plausible minor-phase tests because several of their strongest lines overlap; I’ll establish a converged ZnWO₄ baseline first, then test each addition from an identical saved state.

The first refinement exposed and removed an exact scale redundancy (histogram and phase scales had both been free). With the histogram scale fixed, the single-phase model is converging cleanly; the current broadening terms are strongly correlated, so I will simplify them before accepting any profile branch.

Matched profile branches reject the negative-Lorentzian solutions and the over-parameterized U/V/W model. A two-term Gaussian model (U+W) is the smallest stable choice with positive calculated widths; adding Lorentzian X improves Rwp by only 0.0035 percentage point, below the branch-selection margin. I’m now testing crystallite size and microstrain independently against that selected baseline.

The phase tests show two real residual components, not just profile mismatch: wurtzite ZnO gives a positive ~8 wt% contribution, while room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ drops Rwp from 18.82% to 13.41% and accounts for ~17 wt%. A triclinic WO₃ entry reaches a slightly lower Rwp only by collapsing its literature metric toward monoclinic symmetry, so I’m treating that as an over-flexible alternative and retesting all WO₃ polymorphs from the updated ZnWO₄+ZnO baseline.

The three-phase inventory is stable at roughly 77% ZnWO₄, 16% monoclinic WO₃, and 8% ZnO. Re-running the angular and global-profile comparisons keeps the displacement-only angular model and U+W Gaussian broadening; the more complex Lorentzian branch develops negative total widths. I’m now performing the required phase-by-phase size and strain tests.

Only isotropic size broadening for ZnWO₄ survives removal testing (about 0.68 μm); all strain terms go negative or add less than the 0.005-point margin, and size terms for ZnO/WO₃ are unsupported. I’m screening simple March–Dollase axes one phase at a time next, because the remaining mismatch is chiefly relative peak intensity rather than centroid or width error.

The phase inventory is now strongly narrowed to ZnWO₄ + ZnO + monoclinic WO₃. I’m finishing the required mature-model retests now: texture/envelope competition, coordinate stability, leave-one-out support, and replay-safe export/audits.

The added texture tests are already showing a useful distinction: weak preferred orientation in ZnWO₄ is real, while extra orientation terms for the minor phases offer only small gains. I’m letting the remaining matched branches finish before deciding whether any survive the parsimony check.

The envelope comparison uncovered a large but physically problematic intensity trend: unconstrained polarization drives to 1.37 (outside its physical 0–1 range), and surface roughness produces a dropped crystallite-size parameter. I’m rejecting those unconstrained solutions and testing physical boundary/simple variants so the low Rwp cannot masquerade as a valid model.

The physical surface-roughness branch is stable and cuts Rwp from 12.50% to 5.97%; the competing polarization branch reaches only 7.10% at its physical limit and wants to run beyond it. After introducing roughness, the minor-phase texture terms collapse to unity and are removed; only a weak ZnWO₄ [100] March–Dollase term remains supported. Next I’m testing whether atomic coordinates are actually determined by the powder data.

The replay audit now passes after extending the refinement to all 8,301 measured points; saved and freshly regenerated profiles agree to 1.4×10⁻⁸ Rwp. ZnWO₄ coordinates and the two W positions in WO₃ are stable and geometrically plausible; ZnO’s oxygen coordinate gives no material gain, while WO₃ oxygen refinement oscillates and is being kept fixed.

A Lorentzian X term is clearly supported (Rwp 5.81% → 5.57%, positive width across the range, maximum correlation 0.854). Adding Gaussian V is rejected for 0.981 U–V correlation; Y is inferior to X. I’m now rechecking phase-specific size/strain and any second profile term from the X-refined baseline.

The mature model keeps only physically supported broadening: ZnWO₄ size ≈0.42 µm and WO₃ microstrain ≈4.05×10⁻³. Other phase-size trials are unstable or negligible, and extra microstrain terms go negative. I’m repeating structural alternatives and leave-one-out tests from a matched no-size checkpoint to prevent that marginal size term from biasing phase rejection.

The phase replacements are now decisively separated on the same no-size nuisance model: monoclinic WO₃ gives Rwp 5.20%, versus 5.36% for the near-metric triclinic candidate, 8.14% orthorhombic, and 13.32% tetragonal. Leave-one-out Rwp rises to 6.70% without ZnO and 15.04% without WO₃; removing ZnWO₄ destroys the fit. I’m freezing and exporting the selected model now.

One final structural check remains: the WO₃ source did not report displacement parameters, so GSAS-II imported zeros. I’m testing two constrained, chemically sensible WO₃ ADPs (one common W and one common O value); I’ll keep them only if they refine positive and stably without degrading the mature model.

The constrained WO₃ ADP trial stayed positive but coupled at 96–99% to the W coordinates and did not fully settle, so those ADPs are not independently measurable. I’m checking a fixed nominal W/O ADP model instead; this avoids exporting physically meaningless zero placeholders while keeping the limitation explicit.

The fixed nominal WO₃ ADPs are stable and slightly improve the fit, while the previously marginal ZnWO₄ size term loses support (ΔRwp only 0.0017%) and is removed. The cleaner model has no >0.95 correlation. I’m doing the required post-ADP nuisance and phase-replacement pass before re-exporting.

The final model is now frozen at 73.8 wt% ZnWO₄, 7.0 wt% ZnO, and 19.2 wt% monoclinic WO₃, with Rwp 5.151%. I’m running the last replay, CIF, schema, and cross-file consistency checks on the re-exported files.

The model and deliverables are assembled. I’m rerunning the final replay, CIF, and cross-file audits in a compact form so the submitted files are verified against the exact saved project.

The saved GSAS-II project replays to the exported profile within \(1.3\times10^{-8}\) Rwp percentage points, converges without dropped variables, and all three CIF geometry/occupancy screens pass. I’m doing the last numerical consistency check across `final.gpx`, `result.json`, and `fit.csv`.

